2|0@science^1440909622#cukgha6:hibob2$:OK. Yes, with willpower, people can decide to eat less and subsequently lose weight. But the argument isn't whether willpower can direct weight loss (though the evidence seems to show that it is a very unsuccessful plan for the vast majority of people) &gt;why so many people started eating too much **only in the last 30 years or so.** We can be fairly sire that willpower isn't it, though. It's about what changed in the past 30 years to make the western world so much more obese. If your answer is that people have less willpower now, then you are positing that there was a drastic and pretty uniform shift in the psychology of people over those 30 years. The other arguments (changes in the foods readily available in our environment, changes in activity levels) seems much better supported. At the population level, recommending willpower as a method for losing weight is even less successful than abstinence education is as a method of preventing teen pregnancy.
0|0@science^1440936664#cuko914:41145and6$:It's literally the only option unless you think we're gonna find a way to make the things people eat not count. 
1|0@science^1440944741#cukrbx9:hibob2$:Which is like saying abstinence is literally the only option for preventing pregnancy. But I hear you. Right now, there are moderately successful methods that can blunt the need for willpower when it comes to maintaining/losing weight. But if we really want to see people lose weight at the population level we would need a drug that manipulates appetite, probably targeting orexigenic/anorexigenic hormone systems.
