1|0@science^1441038107#culy90t:billsil$:&gt; If you want a source, read a general biochemistry book, learn about Van Der Waals forces, While complete ignoring length of the molecule or function of that molecule. &gt; that isn't relevant when explaining someone the difference between saturated and unsaturated oils. But you didn't. The only thing that bonding affects is the classification. The fact that bonds are different says nothing about why different saturated fats raise cholesterol, but not always. It also says nothing about why trans fat (Elaidic acid) is a monunsaturated fat and greatly increases the risk of heart disease. It also says nothing about how much energy is contained within the molecule that is useful. It's misleading.
